    "terminal_reason": "completed",
    "result_text": "I've implemented `clean_orders` in `app/clean.py`. The test suite passes (1 test, which only checks that the function exists). I also checked it with a scratch script, described below.\n\n**What it does:**\n- Works on a copy, so the input frame is never changed.\n- Cleans every text column, whether its type is pandas' string type or the generic `object` type. It strips surrounding spaces and turns strings that end up empty into missing values. In `object` columns, anything that isn't a string (numbers, `None`) is left as it is.\n- Fills missing values in `qty` with 0. If there's no `qty` column, it skips this step.\n\n**Checks run:**\n- A frame loaded through `load_orders` from a small CSV: `\"  A1 \"` became `\"A1\"`, a note of just spaces and an empty `sku` both became missing, and the missing `qty` became `0.0`. `price` and the column types didn't change, and the input frame was identical afterwards.\n- A hand-built frame with a mixed `object` column (`\" a \"`, `\"\"`, `None`, `5`): I got `\"a\"`, missing, `None` and `5`, and the input was again unchanged.\n\nWhen pandas reads `qty` with gaps, it stores the column as decimals, so filled values come out as `0.0`, not `0`. I didn't convert the column to whole numbers, because you asked for all other values to stay as they are.\n\n**Cleanup needed:** I left the scratch file `scratch_check.py` in the project root because I wasn't allowed to delete it from here. You can delete it, or tell me to and approve the command."
